-- title:
--   Descent of a finite-level GL₂ Galois representation to a finite field
-- statement:
--   Let $\kappa$ be a finite field, $\Omega$ an algebraically closed field, $\iota\colon\kappa\to\Omega$ a ring homomorphism, and $\rho\colon\Gamma_{\mathbb Q}=\mathrm{Aut}_{\mathbb Q}(\overline{\mathbb Q})\to\mathrm{GL}_2(\Omega)$ a group homomorphism, where $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ is the algebraic closure of $\mathbb Q$. Assume $\rho$ factors through a finite level: there is an intermediate field $L$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q$, finite-dimensional over $\mathbb Q$, with $\rho(\sigma)=1$ for every $\sigma$ fixing $L$ pointwise. Let $S$ be a finite set of natural numbers and assume that for every prime $p\notin S$, every valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $p$ a nonunit of $A$, and every $\sigma\in\Gamma_{\mathbb Q}$ lying in the decomposition subgroup of $A$ over $\mathbb Q$ and acting on the residue field of $A$ by $x\mapsto x^{p}$, both $\mathrm{tr}\,\rho(\sigma)$ and $\det\rho(\sigma)$ lie in the image of $\iota$. The conclusion is the existence of a homomorphism $\rho_0\colon\Gamma_{\mathbb Q}\to\mathrm{GL}_2(\kappa)$ which again factors through a finite level in the same sense, whose associated $\kappa$-linear representation on $\kappa^{2}$ (via [`Deformation.matrixRepresentation`](def/Deformations_MatrixRepresentation.html#L15)) is semisimple, which satisfies $\rho_0(\sigma)=1$ whenever $\rho(\sigma)=1$, and for which the characteristic polynomial of the matrix $\iota(\rho_0(\sigma))$ equals that of $\rho(\sigma)$ in $\Omega[X]$, for every $\sigma\in\Gamma_{\mathbb Q}$.
--
--   This is the descent step of Deligne–Serre (the passage from their Théorème 6.1 to Théorème 6.7): the semisimplification of $\rho$ admits a model over the finite field $\kappa$ generated by the Frobenius traces and determinants, with unchanged characteristic polynomials and a kernel at least as large. It is used in the construction of the residual mod-$\ell$ representation attached to a weight-two Hecke eigenform.
